about your environment**\n- **ESLint Version: v3.8.1**\n- **Node Version: v4.6.0**\n- **npm Version: 3.10.9**\n\nSelection or rules from `.eslintrc.json`:\n\n```\n    \"extends\": \"eslint:recommended\",\n    \"parserOptions\": {\n        \"ecmaVersion\": 6,\n        \"sourceType\": \"module\",\n        \"ecmaFeatures\": {\n            \"impliedStrict\": true,\n            \"jsx\": true,\n            \"experimentalObjectRestSpread\": true\n        }\n    },\n    \"env\": {\n        \"browser\": true\n    },\n```\n\n**What did you do? What did you expect to happen? What actually happened?**\n\nSee screenshot:\n\n![image](https://cloud.githubusercontent.com/assets/259840/19639168/8dcbc924-99d7-11e6-8e8d-ade0f88a48f1.png)\n\nSpotted in [VSCode 1.6.1](https://code.visualstudio.com/updates), using [ESlint plug-in](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=dbaeumer.vscode-eslint).\n\nTyped arrays are [part of the ES6 standard](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Int32Array), and I've set my environment to browser too, so I think this should not be triggering `no-undef`, right?\n\nI did a quick search, [it seems this issue has been raised and fixed in the past](https://github.com/eslint/eslint/issues/846) so somewhere a regression must have slipped in (or I'm screwing up my ESLint configuration somehow, that's of course also an option).\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/eslint/eslint/issues/7440/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/eslint/eslint/issues/7440/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":"completed"}},"event":"cross-referenced"},{"id":1461480329,"node_id":"MDExOkxvY2tlZEV2ZW50MTQ2MTQ4MDMyOQ==","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/eslint/eslint/issues/events/1461480329","actor":{"login":"eslint-deprecated[bot]","id":29966797,"node_id":"MDM6Qm90Mjk5NjY3OTc=","avatar_